In dropInvalidText, normalise once and store what was judged — the read-path form of SettingRow's 'commit exactly what was VALIDATED' rule:

```ts
for (const [path, isValid] of Object.entries(textValidators)) {
  const value = readSettingValue(settings, path);
  if (value === undefined) continue;
  // The SAME form both validators judge: trimmed, with every whitespace run a single space.
  // AlphaTab's own parsers read the RAW string — Color.fromJson tests startsWith('#') against it,
  // and FontParser splits on a literal ' ' only, so a tab between the size and the family reaches
  // it as one token and throws 'Missing font list'.
  const normalised = typeof value === 'string' ? value.trim().replaceAll(/\s+/g, ' ') : value;
  if (typeof normalised !== 'string' || !isValid(normalised)) {
    settings = writeSettingValue(settings, path, readSettingValue(defaults, path) ?? '');
    repaired.push(path);
  } else if (normalised !== value) {
    settings = writeSettingValue(settings, path, normalised);
    repaired.push(path);
  }
}
```

ASSUMPTION I am naming so it can be overridden: the normalised-but-valid case is pushed to `repaired`, which makes storage self-healing (the effect at PlayerShell.tsx:195 rewrites the cleaned document) at the cost of one toast naming a row whose rendered value did not visibly change. If that reads as crying wolf, drop the `repaired.push` in the `else if` only — the engine is still protected, the raw value simply stays in storage and is re-normalised on every visit, which is idempotent and harmless.
